Track 3: Degaussing the Sleeper Pins
- Story & Context: Legend spread across the Silesian railyards that a deserted gravel switchpoint called Neuss-Nord retained rhythmic electromagnetic charges from hundreds of commuter synth jams, permanently vibrating the iron ties beneath the ballast. Senior track inspector Boro discovered the phenomenon when his handheld magnetic testing wand went haywire, emitting blinding barrages of 32nd-note clock clicks whenever he approached the concrete ties of spur line four. Kneeling in the bitter morning frost as his headlamp flickered out, he documented the ground humming in perfect synchronization with long-gone night commuters, capturing an inexplicable ghostly phrase echoing from an uncoupled freight tanker: "We left our teeth inside the railbed." His official maintenance report was dismissed as sleep-deprivation delirium, but illegal commuter trains still cut their diesel engines at that exact coordinate to play back the ground's metallic resonance.
